exports[`every prompt the game paints with > normal maps, the style judge and a kit texture sheet > castle-stone 1`] = `
"This picture is a layout guide for a game texture sheet. Repaint it as the finished sheet: keep the magenta background exactly as it is, and fill every green shape completely, right to its exact edges, with the texture described for it below. Keep every shape exactly where it is and exactly its size and outline; do not move, shrink, enlarge or round any shape. Paint nothing outside the green shapes and leave no green anywhere.

Every texture is flat and seen square-on, like a scan of a flat surface held straight in front of the camera: no perspective, no isometric view, no 3D, no depth, no objects drawn on top. Built from a few flat facets of solid colour with crisp edges and very low contrast, soft and muted, in the colours each texture below names. No lighting at all: no shading, no shadows, no highlights, no gradients, no darker edges or corners, no glow; the same brightness all over each texture. No border, frame or outline round any shape. No writing, letters or symbols.

1. The tall rectangle in the top row, first from the left: one outside wall section of an old forest castle, between two posts: rough grey-green stone blocks with a few soft patches of moss low down.

2. The tall rectangle in the top row, second from the left: the same wall seen from inside the castle hall: grey-green stone blocks on the upper two thirds, and dark oak panelling on the lower third.

3. The round-topped arch in the top row, third from the left: a great castle gate of heavy dark oak planks filling the whole arch, with broad black iron straps across it and big round iron studs.

4. The square in the top row, fourth from the left: a narrow castle window: four small panes of flat even pale blue-grey glass with no reflections, divided by a slim dark iron cross that reaches all four edges; no outer frame.

5. The square in the top row, fifth from the left: plain rough grey-green stone with a few very faint flat facets, nothing else.

6. The square in the bottom row, first from the left: a castle floor of large worn grey flagstones of different sizes, four rows from top to bottom, with thin even joints.

7. The wide rectangle in the bottom row, second from the left: slate roof tiles in rows running from left to right, flat rectangular slates in soft slate blue and blue-grey, six rows from top to bottom, each row overlapping the one below it, joints offset from row to row.

8. The wide rectangle in the bottom row, third from the left, the upper one: a castle wall of big rough grey-green stone blocks, four rows from top to bottom, four or five blocks in each row, joints offset from row to row, thin pale mortar lines all equally thick.

9. The long thin strip in the bottom row, third from the left, the lower one: one dark oak beam: dark brown wood with a long grain running from left to right along the strip.

Textures 5, 6, 7, 8, 9 are repeated side by side and one above another in the game, so keep every shape the same size all over and the lines between rows and shapes the same thickness all over."
`;

exports[`every prompt the game paints with > normal maps, the style judge and a kit texture sheet > timber-cottage 1`] = `
"This picture is a layout guide for a game texture sheet. Repaint it as the finished sheet: keep the magenta background exactly as it is, and fill every green shape completely, right to its exact edges, with the texture described for it below. Keep every shape exactly where it is and exactly its size and outline; do not move, shrink, enlarge or round any shape. Paint nothing outside the green shapes and leave no green anywhere.

Every texture is flat and seen square-on, like a scan of a flat surface held straight in front of the camera: no perspective, no isometric view, no 3D, no depth, no objects drawn on top. Built from a few flat facets of solid colour with crisp edges and very low contrast, soft and muted, in the colours each texture below names. No lighting at all: no shading, no shadows, no highlights, no gradients, no darker edges or corners, no glow; the same brightness all over each texture. No border, frame or outline round any shape. No writing, letters or symbols.

1. The tall rectangle in the top row, first from the left: one outside wall section of a timber-framed cottage, between two posts: cream plaster with one slim warm-brown timber brace running diagonally from its lower left corner to its upper right corner.

2. The tall rectangle in the top row, second from the left: the same wall seen from inside the room: plain warm cream plaster on the upper two thirds, and simple upright honey-brown boards on the lower third.

3. The round-topped arch in the top row, third from the left: a front door made of upright sage-green planks filling the whole arch, with two dark iron straps across it and a small iron ring handle on the right.

4. The square in the top row, fourth from the left: a window: four panes of glass, each one flat even pale blue-grey with no reflections, glints or streaks, divided by a slim warm-brown timber cross that reaches all four edges; no outer frame.

5. The square in the top row, fifth from the left: plain cream plaster with a few very faint flat facets, nothing else.

6. The square in the bottom row, first from the left: wooden floor boards running from left to right, honey-brown, six boards from top to bottom, each board split into planks of different lengths, with plank ends staggered from board to board.

7. The wide rectangle in the bottom row, second from the left: roof shingles in rows running from left to right, rectangular flat shingles in dusty rose and terracotta, six rows from top to bottom, each row overlapping the one below it, joints offset from row to row.

8. The wide rectangle in the bottom row, third from the left, the upper one: a stone wall of rounded rectangular blocks in warm grey and sand only (no other colours), four rows of blocks from top to bottom, five or six blocks in each row, with the joints offset from row to row; thin pale mortar lines between the rows and between the blocks, all equally thick.

9. The long thin strip in the bottom row, third from the left, the lower one: one timber beam: warm brown wood with a long grain running from left to right along the strip.

Textures 5, 6, 7, 8, 9 are repeated side by side and one above another in the game, so keep every shape the same size all over and the lines between rows and shapes the same thickness all over."
`;

exports[`every prompt the game paints with > normal maps, the style judge and a kit texture sheet 1`] = `
"Make the tangent-space normal map of the attached game picture, for 2D lighting.
Exactly the same shapes, outline and size as the picture, every detail in the same place, filling the same frame.
Colours mean the direction each surface faces: flat facing the viewer is lavender blue (128, 128, 255); facing right is pinker, facing left is bluer-green; facing the top of the picture is greener, facing the bottom is more purple.
Every flat facet is one even colour for the one way it faces, with crisp edges where facets meet; nothing is rounded or bumpy.
Where the picture is empty or magenta, fill with flat lavender blue (128, 128, 255).
No shading, no light, no colours of the picture itself, no outlines, no writing.
The picture shows: Glowing Lantern."
`;

exports[`every prompt the game paints with > normal maps, the style judge and a kit texture sheet 2`] = `
"You check game art for a stylised low-poly isometric fantasy RPG.
The first pictures are approved reference sprites that set the style. The last picture is a new sprite to check.
Answer two questions about the new sprite:
1. subject: does it show the thing described (its kind and its most recognisable feature)? Small differences in detail are fine.
2. style: does it look like it belongs with the references: flat faceted shapes with crisp edges, soft muted pastels, no outlines or texture, no light or shadow painted in, seen from the same isometric corner?
Fail style only for clear problems: a photo or 3D render look, anime cel shading, flat vector art, writing or letters, several subjects or a sprite sheet, or scenery behind the subject.
Reply with JSON: {"subject": true or false, "style": true or false, "problem": "one short plain sentence, empty when both are true"}."
`;

